<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Standalone app. Nothing here is decorative: AMFI parses this file at sign
  time and rejects anything it does not like, including a comment placed above the DOCTYPE.

  jamin compiles the chart by running its own JavaScript in JavaScriptCore, which needs to write
  machine code and run it. Under the hardened runtime that is exactly what is forbidden unless
  this entitlement says otherwise. Without it JSC does not fail loudly -- it quietly falls back to
  its interpreter, and the only symptom is a compile that takes longer than it should, which is
  the kind of thing nobody traces back to a missing line in a plist.

  The AU and VST3 bundles get no entitlements file at all. A plugin runs inside its host's process
  and inherits the host's entitlements, so an empty dict would be exactly equivalent to omitting
  the flag -- and one fewer file is one fewer thing that can fail to parse. What they do need is
  the hardened runtime, which is a codesign flag rather than an entitlement.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
